  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1026 PM CDT SUN AUG 30 2026\n\nUpper level ridging is beginning to take control of the region\nlate this evening. Water vapor imagery reveals this stout upper\nlevel feature expanding east across much of the southeastern US.\nTo our south, an area of low pressure over the Northern Gulf will\ncontinue to move west over the next couple of days. The NHC has\nincreased development chances to 70% over the next 48 hours as\nthis system nears southwest Louisiana/Upper Texas coastline.  As\nwe head through the work week, the upper ridge will continue to\nexpand east. This will help to steer the tropical low to the west\nacross the Gulf. Drier air and increased subsidence will help\nmaintain rain-free conditions across the northern half of the\narea. We look to continue with low chances for diurnally driven\nconvection across our southern areas where moisture remains\nelevated closer to the coast. At this time, we are not expecting\nmuch in the way of severe weather as wind shear will be minimal.\nHowever, instability will be sufficient enough to support a\nstronger storm or two as we reach peak heating.\n\nA warming trend kicks in over the coming days as temperatures\nclimb back into the mid to upper 90s by mid week. Humid\nconditions will push heat indices into the 105-108 range each \nafternoon. Daily Heat Advisories will likely be needed by Tuesday \nor Wednesday. Overall, this will promote a significant heat risk \nfor the region. The latest probabilistic heat risk data suggests a\n50-60% chance of a major or higher heat risk as we head into \nLabor Day Weekend. Heat safety messaging will be ramped up through\nthe week.\n\n95/Castillo",
